      "content": "Yes, the mean (26.89) is greater than the median (25). When the mean is pulled to the right by larger values—like that 75 in your data set—it indicates that the distribution is skewed to the right.\n\n**Final Answer:** Skewed to the right\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Concept:** Skewness is determined by the relationship between the mean and the median.\n* **What the terms mean:**\n  * **Mean:** The arithmetic average of all data points.\n  * **Median:** The middle value when data is ordered from smallest to largest.\n* **Why we use it:** If the mean is greater than the median, it means there are high-value outliers (like 75) pulling the average up, creating a long 'tail' on the right side of the distribution.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check for outliers first! A single very large or very small value often dictates the skewness of the entire data set."
